## 1. Payments: Whish, OMT, settlement and payout architecture

### Decision

**Contact Whish first for a bounded merchant pilot; keep OMT as a provider-contact/closed-pilot option.** This is a prioritization decision, not a finding that either provider has approved Alo King or offers an open API.

### Verified facts and what they mean

- Whish describes **Whish Pay as a wallet payment gateway for online merchants**, with integrations including Shopify, WordPress, and custom websites. Its corporate materials describe tailored checkout, collection services, payroll, and bulk payments. [1] [2] [3]
- Whish’s official materials describe **payment links** in which the payer enters card details and funds a Whish Wallet; the Send & Receive material specifically mentions sharing payment links through SMS or WhatsApp. Whish terms state that card-funded transactions may undergo automated fraud checks and manual review and may take **up to one business day** to be verified and credited. [3] [4]
- Whish’s published fee page lists **3% for international-card funding** and **3% through a payment link**, with local-card funding shown as free. These are published wallet-service fees—not a confirmed Alo King merchant discount rate, settlement fee, payout fee, or tax-inclusive price. [5]
- OMT Pay publicly describes a **dual-currency LBP/USD wallet**, local and international card funding, payment links, QR store payments, and wallet-to-wallet transfers. OMT’s public corporate Cash Out page establishes that a business service exists, but the reviewed page does not publish API mechanics, test access, batch semantics, or a corporate contract. [6] [7]
- OMT’s consumer Cash Access page gives a **USD 5,000 per-transaction maximum** and a **4.5% fee subject to a USD 9 minimum** for its described e-voucher cash-out flow. That is not evidence of the fee, limit, or process for corporate Alo King settlement. [8]
- BdL Basic Circular No. 1 / Decision 13790, dated **9 January 2026**, defines payment-gateway activity in the EPSP framework and addresses payment accounts, AML/KYC, beneficial-owner due diligence, and prior approval for covered categories. The BdL supervised-company list includes OMT SAL and Whish Money SAL as electronic transfer companies. [9] [10]
- BdL’s index lists **Circular 777 / Decision 13846 dated 21 September 2026**. The available English Basic Circular 1 PDF expressly says that it excludes the Circular 777 amendments, so current rules must be reconciled before launch. BdL’s detail page also lists **Basic Circular 173 / Decision 13810 dated 28 April 2026** on a merchant discount-rate cap; the numerical cap was not exposed in the extracted material. [11] [12]

### Required payment design

Implement a provider adapter and an immutable double-entry ledger before production. At minimum, retain:

- provider and merchant-account identifier;
- Alo King order/reference and provider transaction/reference;
- gross amount, **currency**, fees, expected net, and settlement currency;
- authorization/payment status, provider-authoritative status timestamp, webhook event ID, and idempotency key;
- reversals, refunds, chargebacks, payout batches, settlement date, and settlement evidence;
- payer, recipient, delivery address, and merchant/vendor references as separate entities.

An order becomes **paid** only after a provider-authoritative status or signed webhook is verified server-side and the amount/currency match the order, or after a provider reconciliation process confirms it. A browser redirect, WhatsApp message, or customer screenshot is never sufficient.

Keep USD and LBP as separate ledger currencies. Do not silently convert, round, or absorb FX differences in the Alo King application. Send only **provider-generated payment URLs** through Kapso; never request card numbers, CVVs, OTPs, wallet PINs, or identity documents in WhatsApp.

### Provider-confirmation items

Before engineering scope is approved, ask each candidate provider to confirm:

- merchant-of-record eligibility for Alo King SARL and whether the service is a gateway, collection, payment-facilitation, or another regulated activity;
- sandbox credentials, API reference, authentication method, IP allow-list/mTLS requirements, rate limits, key rotation, idempotency, and production cutover;
- webhook registration, signing algorithm, replay protection, event schema, retry behavior, ordering, and reconciliation/report endpoints;
- supported LBP/USD checkout and settlement currencies, FX source and lock time, rounding/minimums, cut-off, reserves, bank settlement timing, holidays, and all MDR/payment-link/refund/chargeback fees;
- authorization, capture, void, reversal, refund, partial refund, dispute, and failed-payment semantics;
- corporate disbursement or batch-payout API, beneficiary KYC, payout returns/failures, driver/vendor onboarding, and whether any split, connected-account, escrow, or delayed-capture model is supported;
- data processing, retention, subprocessors, audit evidence, and customer-support/SLA commitments.

### Launch boundary

Use one merchant-of-record account and a manually approved payout queue. Treat **marketplace split, escrow, automatic vendor allocation, and stored-value/wallet features as out of scope** until both provider and Lebanese counsel confirm the model.

