## 3. Meta WhatsApp pricing and Free Entry Point

### Effective rule set

Meta’s developer pricing documentation was updated **30 September 2026** and states that the October rate card is effective **1 October 2026**, by Messaging-account timezone. Meta charges only delivered outbound business-to-user messages, by recipient country calling-code market and category. Inbound user-to-business messages are not charged. [25]

From 1 October 2026:

- Marketing, Utility, and Authentication templates remain category-priced.
- Service messages are priced per delivered message after the free tier; the Service rate equals the market’s Utility/Authentication rate and has no Service volume tiers.
- Utility templates sent inside the open 24-hour customer-service window are billable under the updated rule; do not model all ordinary replies as permanently free. [26]
- Each business phone number gets **1,000 delivered Service messages free per calendar month**. Unused units do not roll over; the allowance resets at midnight in the Messaging-account timezone, and billing starts with the 1,001st Service delivery for that number. [25]
- The latest developer pricing page says a Free Entry Point (FEP) window may remain open **up to seven days** when a user reaches the business through a Click-to-WhatsApp ad and Alo King responds within the customer-service window. During an open FEP window, Marketing, Utility, Authentication, and Service deliveries are free; Meta Business Agent messages are not. The WhatsApp Business marketing page still shows older 72-hour/three-day wording, so live-account testing is required. [25] [27] [28]
- Meta Business Agent is a separate category introduced in 2026 and is charged at **$2.00 per 1 million input/output tokens**. Alo King’s own Kapso/Anthropic/OpenAI stack should be treated as Service, not Meta Business Agent, unless Meta’s own Agent Platform powers the interaction. [25]

Instrument webhook pricing fields and analytics by `pricing_category`, `pricing_type`, recipient market, phone number, Messaging account, FEP state, and business/vendor. For Service, distinguish `free_customer_service` events from paid Service events.

