## 6. Lebanese diaspora gifting and cross-border payments

### Demand and product evidence

Kafeel publicly offers weekly/on-demand Lebanese grocery baskets, custom lists, baby supplies, holiday baskets, broad Lebanon coverage, and photo proof. Toters publicly supports restaurant/grocery delivery, scheduled slots, saved addresses/payment, major cards, live tracking, and delivery prices that vary by store, hour, and location. Spinneys Lebanon advertises more than 9,000 products, seven-day slots, refrigerated delivery, and cash-on-delivery, card-on-delivery, and online-gateway payment options. These establish product patterns, not Alo King demand or conversion rates. [51] [52] [53]

WorldRemit’s US-to-Lebanon corridor accepts bank, debit-card, or credit-card funding, shows fees and exchange rate upfront, and supports cash pickup, bank, or mobile-money receipt depending on method. OMT’s public Western Union price list shows $5 for $1–50, $10 for $50.01–200, $15 for $200.01–500, $20 for $500.01–1,000, $35 for $1,000.01–2,000, $70 for $2,000.01–3,000, and $100 for $3,000.01–7,500; those are remittance fees, not Alo King order-payment fees. [54] [55]

The U.S. International Trade Administration cites a World Bank estimate that Lebanon’s cash economy was 45.7% of GDP in 2022 and describes more than one million digital accounts and stated individual digital-payment limits of $300 daily and $3,000 monthly as of March 2025. L’Orient Today reported BdL’s 2026 press-conference figures: card-payment transaction count up 130% in the first eight months of 2026 year-over-year, value up more than 50%, restaurant card share rising from 15% to 35%, large-supermarket share from 17% to 36%, medium-supermarket share from 7.5% to 20%, and online debit-card payments above 51%. These are useful directional context, not a forecast for Alo King. [56] [57]

### Payment recommendation

Use a PSP abstraction and obtain Lebanon-specific written terms. **Tap is the first provider-contact candidate** because its Lebanon page advertises local payments, PCI compliance, and payment links through WhatsApp, SMS, Instagram, and email; the page does not prove merchant eligibility, foreign-card acceptance, pricing, settlement, or marketplace support. [59]

Do not make direct Stripe onboarding for a Lebanon-registered entity a launch dependency: Stripe’s official supported merchant-country list does not include Lebanon. Any foreign-entity/acquiring workaround is a legal, tax, KYC, merchant-of-record, and settlement decision—not a technical shortcut. [58]

Model sender, recipient, payer, and delivery address as separate entities. Show one pre-authorization total containing goods, delivery, service/payment fee, currency/FX, and any substitution reserve. Make refunds, substitutions, price differences, and failed delivery explicit. Use prepaid sender-funded orders as the primary diaspora flow; retain COD/card-on-delivery only when the sender expressly selects it and the recipient confirms.

### Fraud, disputes and evidence

Use CVV, 3DS/step-up where supported, tokenization, velocity/device/IP/geolocation signals, and manual review for first orders, high values, rapid repeats, or unusual patterns. Do not automatically reject a normal payer-recipient country mismatch.

Persist an append-only order timeline containing authorization ID, descriptor, item prices, substitution consent, WhatsApp messages, recipient OTP, driver GPS, delivery photo, named receiver, and refund/credit events. Use a stable statement descriptor such as `ALOKING*ORDER123` and repeat it in WhatsApp/email receipts. Visa identifies card-not-present risks including stolen credentials, fake identities, account abuse, and friendly fraud; Mastercard emphasizes merchant evidence for CNP chargebacks and can expose merchants to dispute consequences. [60] [61]

Pilot recurring family baskets, custom grocery lists, and holiday/occasion baskets before a fully indexed marketplace. Track approval rate, manual-review rate, delivery failure, substitution, refund, recipient confirmation, and chargeback/credit metrics by sender country, order value, merchant, and payment rail. No reliable public Lebanon-specific CNP fraud, chargeback, diaspora order-volume, sender-country, average-basket, or recurring-order benchmark was found; do not invent one.

